The '''AC Canal Commission''' is an [[Adonian Community]] oversight body responsible for supervising the operation of the [[Balboa Canal]] and ensuring compliance with the [[Treaty of Areza]]'s provisions on non-discriminatory access and international use. The Commission is headquartered in the [[International City of Areza]], on the north shore of [[Lake Alvarado]] within the canal zone.

The Commission's authority supersedes Balboan domestic law within the canal zone, which extends approximately 10 kilometres on either side of the waterway. It is one of the principal international institutions in Areza and a central element of the AC's oversight of the [[Twelve Guarantees]].

== History ==

=== Mandate era (1950–1973) ===

The AC Canal Commission was originally established in 1950, when the [[Adonian Community]] assumed administration of the canal zone following the end of the [[Zong Balboa]] period. During the mandate era, the Commission had direct operational responsibility for the canal: it managed reconstruction of the war-damaged infrastructure, oversaw the reopening of the waterway to commercial traffic in 1958, and administered the canal's toll and access policies on behalf of the international community.

The mandate-era Commission was composed of representatives of the AC and its major maritime member states, and its decisions were subject to AC approval. The reconstruction programme (1950–1967), which involved the complete rebuilding of the lock systems, the dredging and widening of the channel, and the construction of modern traffic management and pilotage systems, was conducted under the Commission's direction.

=== Treaty of Areza (1973) ===

The [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973) returned the canal to Balboan sovereignty and reconstituted the Commission as an oversight body rather than an operational one. Under the treaty, the [[Balboa Canal Authority]] — a Balboan state agency under the [[Ministry of Interior (Balboa)|Ministry of Interior]] — assumed operational responsibility for the canal, while the Commission retained the right to supervise, monitor, and, where necessary, direct the Authority's actions to ensure treaty compliance.

The reconstitution was a significant change: the Commission gave up its direct management role but gained a permanent legal mandate to oversee the canal's operation, backed by the treaty's provision that its authority supersedes Balboan domestic law within the canal zone. The Commission's role was enshrined in the [[Twelve Guarantees]] as the Eighth Guarantee, which provides for the continued operation of the canal under international oversight.

== Composition ==

The Commission consists of:

* One representative of the [[Adonian Community]], who serves as the Commission's chair.
* One representative of the [[Republic of Balboa]], typically a senior official of the [[Ministry of Interior (Balboa)|Ministry of Interior]] or the [[Balboa Canal Authority]].
* A rotating panel of five representatives of AC member states, selected by the AC and serving staggered terms.

Decisions are taken by majority vote, with the chair holding a casting vote in the event of a tie. The Commission meets in regular session at its headquarters in Areza, and may convene emergency sessions at the request of any member or of the AC Secretary-General.

